---
/**
 * The club's **standing** sign-up paths — the chat, the group, the calendar file — rendered ONCE per page
 * under a lead-in, not once per row.
 *
 * **2026-11-15 (round-4 design brief §6, ruling D-B): this is no longer "the sign-up cluster on a schedule
 *  row". A row carries one action — the per-date sign-up page, painted in `EventRow.astro` from
 *  `splitPaths(paths).row` — and everything the resolver returns besides that is a property of the club,
 *  so it prints once after the last row. The file keeps its name (D173's rule for a handle: a probe handle
 *  does not follow the copy), and every rule below about the resolver, the island, the stamps and the
 *  canonical order still holds; what changed is *how many* pages carry it and which half of the resolver's
 *  destinations is in it.**
 *
 * Why this is a component and not a paragraph inside `EventRow.astro` (S019, D272, 2026-11-10). The operator's
 * ruling is that the site does not hold RSVPs: "the website just needs functionality to make it easy for a
 * user to rsvp at a site that they prefer to rsvp on". So the affordance is a set of links sitting on the row
 * that already prints the date, and the only place a row's set of links can come from is `src/lib/rsvp.mjs`.
 * A component is what makes that import unavoidable: a second copy of the link rule inside the row markup is
 * the same defect class as a second copy inside a gate (D238, D264), and `check-rsvp-page.mjs` compares the
 * painted bytes of this component against `signUpPaths()` output — which is only a meaningful assertion if
 * the markup is generated from that function.
 *
 * The grammar is DESIGN.md's **quiet label**, not a control (D139, D140): `text-label font-medium text-ink`,
 * underline at `decoration-rule`, `min-h-8` (32px), focus ring, **no fill, no keyline, no `data-control`**.
 * A row is one boundary (its own `inset-shadow-rule`, D220). Putting a 48px filled control inside it would
 * draw a second box inside the row's ring — the exact defect `check:render` fails on for notices (D149-3c,
 * design/records/0018) — and four 48px buttons under every date would out-shout the date, which the band is
 * designed to make the loudest thing on the page.
 *
 * Order is not decorative, and it is not the whole row: the resolver's canonical order is Meetup first
 *  (the club's calendar of record for sign-up, D259), then Discord, then Facebook — but what this row paints
 *  is that order MINUS the club-wide exits the site's exit band already paints on every page (round-4 §10's
 *  ceiling on outbound links outside the band, `EXIT_BAND_PLATFORMS` in `src/lib/rsvp.mjs`). On the artifact
 *  today the row therefore carries the Facebook group and the band carries the Meetup group and the Discord
 *  invite. A visitor
 *  who keeps opening one platform gets that platform first from then on (S022, D281) — but
 *  the reorder happens in the browser, in `SignUpLinksCluster.svelte`, and the bytes this file builds are still
 *  the canonical order, so the gate's "what the resolver says" comparison is against the server's answer, not
 *  against one visitor's history. A platform with no **verified** URL is not in the list at all — it is not rendered, and
 *  `scripts/check-rsvp-page.mjs` asserts the absence from the built bytes rather than trusting this file's
 *  silence (S019's open question 3). The same gate asserts the mirror, in the other direction (D280), one
 *  tier wider than this row: a platform `src/config.json` declares must reach a visitor somewhere on the page —
 *  the rows carry Meetup, this row carries what the band does not, and the band's own two exits are asserted by
 *  rule 6c, so a component that quietly drops one is caught as surely as one that invents one.
 *
 * The labels name the platform and the action (D273, 2026-11-11). The operator's ruling was that a visitor
 * should see several ways in, plainly, rather than one button called "Details and sign-up" that happens to go
 * to Meetup: "I want to present many options of platforms on which a visitor can use to RSVP... friendly,
 * informational, open". So each label says which site it opens and what the visitor does there — *Sign up on
 * Meetup*, *Join the Discord chat*, *Join the Facebook group*. A label that names the platform needs no
 * hidden " on Meetup" suffix; a label that is an event title (the `several` state) carries it in the
 * screen-reader text instead, so two lifted-out links are never both called "Proofs".
 *
 * Facebook is the one platform this rig cannot measure. Measured 2026-11-11 (Linux native, curl, browser UA,
 * redirects followed): Facebook answers **400 to every anonymous GET** from here — the club's group
 * (`/groups/matheapolis`), a group that does not exist, and a page alike — so a status code is not evidence
 * for or against any particular URL, and a gate that treated one as such would be checking nothing
 * (D280). The club's group is public and the operator named it (`.notes/SESSIONS.md`, 2026-11-11), so the
 * gate holds that key on the two things it *can* read: `FACEBOOK_GROUP_URL_SHAPE` in `src/lib/rsvp.mjs`, and a
 * dated, named confirmation (`_verified.facebookGroup.confirmedBy` / `confirmedAt`) inside the same 60-day
 * window the probe stamps use. When a probe that can reach Facebook exists, its 2xx/3xx stamp replaces the
 * confirmation; nothing in this markup changes, because the component only ever reads `paths.destinations`.
 *
 * Discord is not just a chat. A guild scheduled event has its own page, `discord.com/events/<guild>/<id>/`,
 * and that is a sign-up surface (D274). Whether one exists for a printed date is not guessed: it is read from
 * the guild's own endpoint into `data/discord-events.json` and matched on the same date + wall-clock hour as
 * a Meetup page. Where the guild has no scheduled event for a date, the Discord link is the invite, labelled
 * as the chat — the site never tells a visitor to RSVP on a platform that has no page for that date.
 * Guild scheduled events are `GuildOnly`, so a non-member landing on one is asked to join the server first;
 * that is why the label is "Sign up on Discord" and not a promise of a one-click attend.
 *
 * The date, the hour and the sign-up pages all come from committed snapshots (`data/events.json`,
 * `data/meetup-events.json`, `data/discord-events.json`) — the build never reaches the network (D73's
 * topology). Both event snapshots carry headcounts upstream; `meetupView()` and `discordView()` in
 * `src/lib/rsvp.mjs` are whitelist projections that drop them, so no count can reach this markup (D272).
 * `check:content` and `check-rsvp-page.mjs` are the two ends of that rule: one stops a count entering the
 * render path, the other reads the painted page and fails if one got there.
 *
 * `data-rsvp-platform`, `data-rsvp-date`, `data-rsvp-hour`, `data-rsvp-state`, `data-rsvp-snapshot`,
 * `data-rsvp-age`, `data-rsvp-discord-snapshot` and `data-rsvp-discord-age` are probe handles for
 * `scripts/check-rsvp-page.mjs`, not styling. `data-rsvp-hour` is what lets the gate re-run the resolver from
 * the painted page alone: the row's own hour is painted as prose ("time not set" when the organizer has not
 * settled it, D169), and a gate that re-parses prose breaks the day the prose is rewritten (D173).
 * They are written per link rather than derived from the row's `data-date` because a cluster can sit in more than
 * one container and a gate that has to know which one it is inside is a gate that breaks when the layout moves
 * (D173). Since D306 (2026-11-14) the only container is the `/schedule` row - the homepage's compact card, which
 * was the second one when this note was written, prints no date and carries no cluster.
 */
import config from '../config.json';
import meetupSnapshot from '../../data/meetup-events.json';
import discordSnapshot from '../../data/discord-events.json';
import { signUpPaths, splitPaths, snapshotAgeDays } from '../lib/rsvp.mjs';
import { ctaSays } from '../lib/links.mjs';
import SignUpLinksCluster from '../components/SignUpLinksCluster.svelte';
import PlatformMarks from './PlatformMarks.astro';
import { spriteFor } from '../lib/platform-marks.mjs';
import type { UpcomingItem } from '../lib/content.ts';

type Props = {
  /** The date the standing row is stamped with — the FIRST date the page prints. The stamps (hour, state,
     snapshot age) describe a date the page actually shows, so the freshness a reader sees under the last row
     is the freshness the rows above were built from, and `check:rsvp-page` can still key the cluster on a
     printed date. */
  item: UpcomingItem;
  /** The page's own words for the standing row, so a page that wants a different lead-in does not fork the
     component. Defaults to the schedule's. */
  lead?: string;
};

const { item, lead = 'Between meetings:' } = Astro.props;
const paths = signUpPaths(item, meetupSnapshot.events, config.URLs, discordSnapshot.events);
// Round-4 brief §6, ruling D-B (2026-11-15): the row's ONE action stayed in the row; everything else the
// resolver returns is a **standing** resource — the chat, the group, the calendar file — and it belongs to
// the club, not to a date. So this component renders it once per page, after the last row, and the split
// happens in `src/lib/rsvp.mjs` (`splitPaths`) rather than here: the gate that compares painted bytes against
// `signUpPaths()` must see the same split the page makes, or it would be comparing the wrong thing (D264).
// The second argument is what makes the split also a **dedup**: `src/lib/rsvp.mjs`'s `EXIT_BAND_PLATFORMS`
// names the club-wide exits the site's exit band already paints on every page (the Meetup group and the
// Discord invite), and a URL already painted at the bottom of the page is not painted a second time under
// the rows. On the artifact today that leaves the standing row one exit — the Facebook group — which is
// §10's "no page contains more than six outbound links outside the footer" at five primaries + one exit
// @measured 2026-11-15 by `npm run check:rsvp-page` (see the standing-row line it prints).
const standing = splitPaths(paths, config.URLs).standing;
// The ages the page gate will see. Stated here so a reader of the built HTML can tell *why* a date has no
// page: `data-rsvp-snapshot` is the stamp, and `check:rsvp` is the gate that fails a build over it.
const age = snapshotAgeDays(meetupSnapshot);
const discordAge = snapshotAgeDays(discordSnapshot);
// MS41 (S090): the sentences this row owes its exits, taken from the registry rather than typed here. A row that
// paints an off-site exit must say what that exit is, and `CALL_TO_ACTION` in `src/lib/links.mjs` is where those
// sentences live — the same strings `/join` paints, which `check:join-page` compares byte for byte. Derived from
// `standing`, so if the band ever stops painting one of these exits and this row picks it up, the sentence arrives
// with it instead of being remembered by hand (D264).
const standingSay = standing.map((d: any) => ctaSays(d.cta)).filter(Boolean);
---

<!-- Round-4 brief §6 and §4, rulings D-B and D341 (2026-11-15): the standing row. Same island, same resolver,
  same canonical order, same remembered-platform reorder — one instance per page instead of one per row, so
  `/schedule` pays five sign-up links plus this row rather than fifteen. The lead-in is prose, the platform
  labels are the resolver's (D273: a label that names the platform needs no hidden suffix), and the calendar
  file is the club's own route, so it carries no `target`, no new tab, and no qualifier (`check:ext-links`
  S041: an internal route is not an exit). The arrow the cluster used to paint after every label is gone
  from the island, not styled away: §4 called fifteen of them "decoration pretending to be affordance", and
  the affordance is the underline plus the sr-only qualifier.
  Since the split also dedups (2026-11-15, §10's six-link ceiling), the chat and the group page this row used
  to repeat are the band's, one tier below — the island is still the thing that can reorder a visitor's
  remembered platform, and `SignUpLinksCluster.svelte` still reads `localStorage`; what changed is that the
  row it feeds now carries the exits the band does not paint. Everything passed below is a primitive or a
  plain array because it is serialised into the island tag: the snapshots and the config never leave the
  server. Astro server-renders the island, so the bytes `check:rsvp-page` reads are produced here at build
  time in the canonical order — the visitor's remembered platform is applied in the browser, never on the
  server, so a JavaScript-off visitor, a crawler and a screen reader all get the order the gate asserts. -->
{spriteFor(Astro.locals) && <PlatformMarks />}
<div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-4 gap-y-2" data-cta-block={`${standing.map((d: any) => d.cta).filter(Boolean).join(' ')} calendar-feed`}>
  <p class="text-label text-ink-3">{lead}</p>
  <SignUpLinksCluster
    client:visible
    destinations={standing}
    note={paths.note}
    date={paths.date}
    signupState={paths.state}
    hour={paths.hour ?? 'unsettled'}
    meetupSnapshot={meetupSnapshot.source.fetchedAt}
    meetupAge={age === null ? 'unknown' : age.toFixed(1)}
    discordSnapshot={discordSnapshot.source?.fetchedAt ?? 'none'}
    discordAge={discordAge === null ? 'unknown' : discordAge.toFixed(1)}
  />
  {/* The calendar file, in the standing row rather than in a second paragraph below it: one path to one file,
      and the sentence under it is about that path. `check:dated-surfaces` and `check:ics` own the bytes of
      the file itself; this is only the way to it.
      MS36 (D444): this anchor wears the calendar path's hue — `text-path-calendar` instead of `text-ink` — so
      the hue that round 2 reserved for "our own /events.ics" (global.css:102) is now painted where that file is,
      which is the one thing the token was reserved for and which nothing shipped until now. The pair is an
      asserted pair, not a hope: `path-calendar on paper` is a row in design/grill/palette-remap.mjs's PAIRS and
      `npm run check:contrast` measures it (4.90, over the 4.5 text bar) on every run. The hover stays the
      stated `hover:text-accent` this anchor already carried — 5.77 on paper, the same pair as the page's other
      quiet links — so the hue carries which path this is at rest, and the hover stays the one motion-colour
      rule the whole page uses. */}
  <a href="/events.ics" data-cta="calendar-feed" class="text-label font-medium text-path-calendar underline decoration-rule underline-offset-2 hover:text-accent focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">Add to a calendar</a>
</div>
{/* MS41 (S090, D467): the row's own context. D407 took the calendar file's sentence out of this row on purpose —
    the exit band carries that one on every emitted page, and printing it twice on `/dates` was the defect — so what
    the row still owed was a sentence about the exit only it paints. This is that sentence, printed from the
    registry (`ctaSays`), not written here: the words a visitor reads and the words `scripts/check-cta-context.mjs`
    counts are one string. It sits inside the marked row, so the block the gate reads is the block the reader sees. */}
{standingSay.length > 0 && (
  <p class="mt-2 max-w-measure text-body leading-prose text-ink-2 font-body">{standingSay.join(' ')}</p>
)}
{/* Round-8 review (2026-11-18), `/dates` Remove "delete line 95 (repeated explanation)": this sentence and the
    band's copy beside it are the same explanation on one page twice. `check:page-exit`'s rule D407 keeps the
    band's copy (the band must not repeat what the page's own nav carries, and the band is standing chrome on
    every emitted page, D115), so the body's copy is the half that goes — deleting the band's instead is the other
    defect, and `check-notice`/`check:page-exit` would then read a band with fewer paths out. Nothing about the
    calendar file is lost here: the path is the link above, its words say what it is, and `/events.ics`'s own bytes
    carry the format claim (`check:ics`'s rules own that file). No anchor is removed, so the page's outbound count
    and the ratified 6-anchor ceiling do not move (`node design/link-ceiling.mjs` prints it). */}

<!-- One standing row per page, and one island per standing row. The cluster itself lives in
  `src/components/SignUpLinksCluster.svelte` (S022, D281): this file decides *what* the standing row offers,
  that component decides *in what order* a particular visitor sees it. Astro server-renders the island, so the
  bytes `check:rsvp-page` reads come out of this file in the canonical order (the resolver's standing half,
  after `splitPaths` dedups the exits the band paints), and the remembered platform is applied only in the
  browser — a JavaScript-off visitor, a crawler and a screen reader all get the order the gate asserts. Everything
  passed below is a primitive or a plain array because it is serialised into the island tag; the snapshots and
  the config never leave the server. `client:visible` is the same strategy the /chat page uses for
  `DiscordSummary.svelte`. -->
